WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
Let's talk about the polls 

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.680
and whether we can 
trust them to pick  

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.200
the next US president. 

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.880
The president is behind — and behind by a lot.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.640
Most national polls 
have Joe Biden

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.600
with a lead over 
Donald Trump. 

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.600
Sound familiar?

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.960
Polls showing Hillary 
Clinton ahead.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.760
This is unheard of in recent presidential politics. 

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.640
Just ask Hillary Clinton how that turned out for her.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.560
So if polls can’t 
predict a winner 

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.400
why are there so 
many of them? 

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.640
Can we trust them? 

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.200
And while we’re 
talking predictions 

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.760
will Trump win again?

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.120
When it comes to picking the winner of a presidential race 

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.640
Allan Lichtman thinks opinion polls are a waste of time.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.520
He says they’re snapshots, 
not crystal balls.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:17.240
Who’s Allan Lichtman?

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.800
Only the guy who’s predicted every winner since 1984.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.440
That’s nine US presidents 
in a row.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.320
He predicted Ronald
Reagan’s win  

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.520
nearly two years before 
the vote. 

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.000
He called Barack Obama’s re-election in 2012 

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.200
when many people thought the race was too close to call.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.360
And against almost 
all the polls 

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.720
he picked Donald 
Trump in 2016.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.800
So who is Lichtman 
picking for 2020? 

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.040
We’ll get to that in a bit. 

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.520
But first let’s talk 
about the polls.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.600
There are national polls, 
state polls  

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.320
and a whole lot of other polls.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.440
And they all do things differently.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.920
US broadcasters like 
CNN and Fox News  

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.480
do their surveys 
over the phone.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.880
Associated Press and the 
Pew Research Center 

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.480
recruit people offline but 
their surveys are online.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.440
Pollsters might ask 
questions like:

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.800
Would you say that your choice of Trump is more

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.200
a vote for Trump or more a vote against Biden?

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.000
But there are many 
 other factors  

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.880
that can influence 
the results.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.760
Things like who paid 
for the poll 

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.760
because that could 
bias the pollster.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.400
How were participants chosen? 

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.760
Where are they from?

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.600
And that’s where something called weighting comes in.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.200
It’s a calculation used by pollsters to make sure

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.080
their sample is representative

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.960
of an area, state or the 
whole country.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.600
They adjust the sample 
for things like

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.880
education, age and gender.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.160
So those are the kinds of things pollsters try to do

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.760
to stay as accurate 
as possible.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.720
But in 2016 a lot 
were off the mark.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.560
Back then some said Clinton’s chances of winning 

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.240
were high.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:09.640
But she didn’t win.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.600
That’s because while Clinton got more votes overall

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.200
what’s called the popular vote

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:17.080
she lost the Electoral College, which is the voting system

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.120
used to decide the presidency. 

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.360
That rarely happens.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.280
And the pollsters didn’t 
see it coming.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.320
So how’d the polls 
get it so wrong?

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.800
Well one of the biggest mistakes in 2016

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.760
happened with state polls. 

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.120
The Pew Research Center
 says there were too many

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.200
cheap-and-fast polls 
and too many 

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.440
college-educated people 
were interviewed.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.320
On Election Day two
 things happened.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.040
First, there was a higher turnout in rural counties 

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.760
and a lower turnout
 in urban ones. 

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.880
That helped Trump.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.600
And second, there were a lot of undecided voters 

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.840
who chose Trump at 
the last minute.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.960
But some of them didn’t 
even get that far.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.920
Research suggests that 
in 2016 a lot of people   

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.120
were discouraged from voting because according to the polls 

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.920
Clinton looked like a shoo-in.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.440
Pollsters say they’ve learned from their mistakes.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:56.440
And they better hope so because the combination

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.240
of Clinton’s loss and
 Trump’s win 

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.200
convinced a lot of people 
that polls are just junk.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.960
But two years later the polls for the US midterms

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.200
helped repair some 
of the damage.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.840
And now the polls are back with a vengeance.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.000
New polling on the 2020 presidential race.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.600
Former vice president 
Joe Biden

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.600
with a lead over 
President Trump.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.000
President Trump behind
 in two states ...

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.720
The poll also shows Biden 
with a 10-point lead …

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:51.720
But David also says a lot 
can still change.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.120
And he’s not alone.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:05.640
That doesn’t mean we 
can’t ever trust polls. 

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.720
There are ways you
 can verify them. 

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.080
And don’t just read one poll. 

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:10.080
Read many.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.120
It’s like getting more 
than one opinion.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.800
The accepted wisdom is that polls do a good job 

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.760
of taking the pulse of
 public opinion.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:21.480
Are people worried about losing their jobs?

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:23.880
Do they care about 
racial inequality?

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:25.880
What about the pandemic 
and their health?

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.880
So let’s bring back Allan Lichtman and find out 

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.960
whether he thinks Trump 
will win again.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.760
He’s created what he 
calls the 13 keys

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:45.240
the keys to the White House.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.560
If Trump loses out on 
six or more

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.280
Lichtman says the 
president is gone.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.920
So, No. 1 is midterm elections.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.680
The Republicans in 
2018 did badly

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.200
so that’s strike one 
against Trump.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:00.240
No. 2 is party nomination.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.000
He didn’t have to fight for it so score one for Trump.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.760
Three is incumbency. 

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.240
Obviously that’s a
 win for Trump. 

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.040
Any third parties running?

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:09.640
Nope. 

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:11.360
He gets another key.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:12.760
The short-term economy? 

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:14.280
Well, the US is in a recession. 

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.040
Bad for the president.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.680
Outlook for the long-term economy?

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.400
Also not good.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.040
Any major policy changes 

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:22.680
from the previous administration?

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.000
There’ve been plenty. 

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.880
Another one for Trump.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:28.160
Key No. 8 is social unrest.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:30.360
And race relations in the
 US are awful

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.880
so Trump loses out there too.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.440
Then there’s scandal. 

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:46.480
Trump takes key No. 10: 

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:49.200
No foreign policy or 
military failures.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.120
But he loses out on No. 11 

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.240
which is foreign policy or military successes.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:12.760
The next one asks whether the incumbent Donald Trump

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:15.000
is inspirational and charismatic.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.800
According to Lichtman he
 is a flashy showman

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:20.800
but he only appeals to a narrow slice of the electorate.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.120
And finally, is the 
challenger Joe Biden  

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.440
charismatic and inspirational?

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:27.880
Lichtman isn’t convinced.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.480
So will Trump remain in 
the White House?

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:38.960
According to Lichtman 
the president  

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.720
has lost out on seven 
of 13 keys.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.520
So he’s pretty confident — but there’s a catch. 

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:54.920
Lichtman says two things could throw out his prediction: 

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.880
Russian hackers, and what he calls voter suppression

00:08:57.880 --> 00:08:59.760
by the Trump administration.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:02.800
And 2020 is far from an ordinary election year

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.520
the pandemic and the 
ruined global economy  

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:06.680
made sure of that. 

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.240
This race is still a 
tough one to call …  

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:13.320
no matter what the polls say.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.120
The original interview with Allan Lichtman 

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.200
was conducted by our colleagues at The Take, 

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.840
which is an Al Jazeera podcast. 

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.040
You can listen to their 
episode about polls

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.640
wherever you get
 your podcasts. 

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:24.240
I’ll see you next week.

